WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
Back in June at AI DevCon in London, one of the recurring themes

2
00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.080
had almost nothing to do with developers.

3
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:09.560
It was about product people, designers and everyone else

4
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:13.200
who was never supposed to open a pull request and now does.

5
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.920
Let's first hear from Marc Sloan of Tessl with the story about an export button

6
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.040
that shows exactly what goes missing when an agent has the ticket

7
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.360
and the design, but none of the context around them.

8
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.480
I'm Marc Sloan, I'm a member of the product

9
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:32.160
team here at Tessl, and I'm here today at a developer

10
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.880
focused conference to talk about non developers.

11
00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:41.240
Now, I don't know if there are any other non developers in the room here today.

12
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.600
I'm thinking yeah there we go.

13
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.840
Product managers, designers executives you know

14
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:50.000
the people who traditionally wouldn't touch code.

15
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.320
We're going to describe a scenario here where we've got a company that has a web

16
00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.800
app product, and a customer has asked the product manager

17
00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:03.920
to put an export button on the dashboard in their product.

18
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.760
Now, the export feature was something that was hidden behind the settings menu,

19
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:12.120
and there was a couple of layers deep, so it was very difficult to find.

20
00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:16.120
Customer wasn't aware that it existed, and requested

21
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.160
this to be in a more prominent position.

22
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:22.200
On the surface, this seems like the perfect

23
00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:27.440
kind of job to give to an Agent Ryan linear ticket that describes it.

24
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.920
Put a figma design in there that shows where the button should go

25
00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:31.920
and what it should look like.

26
00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:33.440
All that good stuff

27
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:37.840
fired off to your agents, have it produce a PR job done, customers happy.

28
00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:38.920
The feature got delivered.

29
00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:40.920
The customer tried it out and said, it's great.

30
00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.960
So cool.

31
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.720
This is the world we live in right now.

32
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.360
We can just send these things off to agents.

33
00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.360
And of course, the agent wasn't building this blind.

34
00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.520
It had access to a bunch of skills that had been developed in advance

35
00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.720
to instruct the agent on how it should be, thinking about the architecture

36
00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.200
of the code, about the front end components that live

37
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.400
within that, the testing and CI requirements,

38
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.840
and how to make use of the API to get the data.

39
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.920
And of course, this is a cutting edge state of the art company.

40
00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.840
So they're using Tesla to manage all of this stuff.

41
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.120
So problem solved.

42
00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.960
Why do we need to be thinking about product and design context?

43
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:27.640
Well in this scenario the agent got one thing wrong.

44
00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:32.680
It actually ended up using a generic react button component

45
00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.120
that lived within the code base,

46
00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.320
and not the more specific export button components.

47
00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.080
That was the right choice here,

48
00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.080
and this might seem like a trivial error,

49
00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:47.040
but encoded within that export button component

50
00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.800
are the accessibility requirements that this organization adheres to.

51
00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:55.760
Interaction patterns that make sense for this kind of async,

52
00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.360
the async operation of this kind of button.

53
00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:02.240
And this was all missed by the agent in creating this.

54
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.000
And what's challenging here is that as far as the harness was concerned,

55
00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.320
everything went smoothly.

56
00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:11.200
The agent got a ticket handed off to it, did the job, created a PR

57
00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:14.560
that passed, and the feature went out to the customer.

58
00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.000
The valuations that were set up on the skills

59
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.760
and the other aspects of the harness all seem to be working well,

60
00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.880
but the product and design context was missing

61
00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.880
from the agent in this particular situation.

62
00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.920
We also heard from Tammuz Dubnov, founder and CEO of autonomy AI,

63
00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:36.040
who has the numbers on this across hundreds of organizations,

64
00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:40.400
74% of pull requests from non-technical people get merged,

65
00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.520
and 84% of those go in without a developer touching them.

66
00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:48.480
So now the the thing that really matters

67
00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.720
for any data scientists in the room is how how do you measure it?

68
00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.400
I'll tell you how we do it across the hundreds of orbs

69
00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.720
and thousands of hours to get opened with us and weekly monthly basis.

70
00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.520
So the first thing to track is, frankly, the count of the pull request.

71
00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.720
How many are open by non-technical individuals and ideally

72
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:10.520
by by which individual.

73
00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:15.600
So you know that every is actually getting a enabled and impactful.

74
00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.920
We have seen places where some non-technical

75
00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:22.480
and are more confident open doctors, some are more hesitant.

76
00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:24.160
You want everybody to actually feel comfortable.

77
00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:28.080
You can track that just by the count, but beyond it, you want to make sure

78
00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.320
that you're not adding PR fatigue by opening nuts and speeds.

79
00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.600
And that's where the merging comes in from.

80
00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:38.120
From our number is just so you're familiar in average

81
00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.200
non-technical contributor and organization.

82
00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.280
In a quarter of openness about 50 there emerging is about 74%.

83
00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:48.400
Which means that one out of four they accidentally overstepped.

84
00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:51.120
And that's okay. That's not PR fatigue.

85
00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.240
That's totally fine, that it's a level where we still have

86
00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.960
a lot of trust between the PRC open and the dev team that reviews them.

87
00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.320
And lastly, you also want to measure how much extra work

88
00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.440
you're adding, not just in terms of PR reviews.

89
00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:09.160
Sometimes developers will need to do follow up work because the agent doesn't

90
00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:09.960
know.

91
00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:13.760
Maybe some future roadmap plans and factors that are intended or whatnot.

92
00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:15.880
And they're how we do this.

93
00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:18.600
We measure from PR that are open and merge.

94
00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.200
How many get merged without any dev interfering,

95
00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.960
without them pushing more commits to fix, change, adjust.

96
00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:28.000
And again, for us that is 84%.

97
00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:32.080
So 84% of the PR merge generated by non-technical individuals

98
00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:33.520
simply get merged,

99
00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:37.280
which is a way to make sure that you are not increasing the burden

100
00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:40.960
on the dev team, not just from PR fatigue, but from a task perspective.

101
00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:44.840
Dana Lawson is CTO at Netlify, and she opened by asking

102
00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:47.840
the room who still identifies as a developer.

103
00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:50.760
Her point is that the builder is now a teacher,

104
00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:54.000
a therapist, a small business owner, a student,

105
00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.120
and building for those agents made the platform better for developers to.

106
00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:01.920
before we get into the architecture, I need to say something

107
00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.160
that might feel uncomfortable.

108
00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:06.200
How many of you here identify as a developer?

109
00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:10.040
Oh man, we ain't precious no more, y'all.

110
00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:13.320
The builder persona has changed.

111
00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:17.520
The person our platforms were built for are no longer just developers.

112
00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:22.080
We're still builders, don't get me wrong, but we're not the only ones.

113
00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.680
Now, with agents, anybody can participate.

114
00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:28.120
In fact, the IDC predicts that more than 500

115
00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:31.680
million new applications will be built by 2028.

116
00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.240
And in fact, I create one a day.

117
00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:39.240
So I'm a I'm a part of that damn problem more than the previous 40 years combined.

118
00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:41.440
Think about it for a minute now,

119
00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:45.280
40 years have been compressed into three, just like this room.

120
00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:47.880
That's not because the number of engineers is expanding.

121
00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:51.600
It's because agents have turned intent into a programing language.

122
00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:54.760
In fact, the strongest programing language that will happen now

123
00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:57.320
and into the future is guess what, English.

124
00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:59.240
So I hope you go and study that in school.

125
00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:02.920
Kids, if you're watching you better, you better learn how to talk to somebody.

126
00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:04.720
Claude Code.

127
00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:06.600
Cursor.

128
00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.320
Bolt. Lovable. Netlify.

129
00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:13.760
These tools let anyone with an idea create working software through conversations.

130
00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.240
No longer is it just developers.

131
00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:18.240
It's therapists, teachers, small business owners, students,

132
00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.040
people in line at the store building experiences on their phone.

133
00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:22.400
Thank you.

134
00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:25.880
Lovable and becoming software

135
00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:29.760
creators at Netlify, we build for all of these builders.

136
00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:30.920
And here's the thing.

137
00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:34.240
When we designed our platform to work better for agents

138
00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:38.240
serving these new builders, it got better for developers too.

139
00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.640
And that's the paradox.

140
00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:43.080
And to close.

141
00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:46.600
Emma Burrows, founder of Rezonant, who was CTO of stripe

142
00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:49.920
in the UK and a product manager at Google before that.

143
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.360
She thinks product managers need to become orchestrators

144
00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.720
the way engineers already have, Which needs a system

145
00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:00.560
that has learned your product and knows the edges of what it knows.

146
00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:04.720
at the moment, where most PMS are, is trying to just kind of do

147
00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:07.800
workflows, basic workflows that help them do their day to day

148
00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:10.960
job, you know, break this down into tickets.

149
00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:13.040
Rezonant also does all of these things very well,

150
00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:17.080
for what it's worth, but I think where we need to go is towards

151
00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:20.880
what software engineers has have become, which is agent orchestrators.

152
00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:25.280
So we identify the slices of product management work

153
00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:29.480
that can actually actually be done autonomously, end to end.

154
00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:30.760
And in order to do that,

155
00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:34.640
you need a system that kind of learns and understands your product.

156
00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:38.840
So most of are here, they're using ChatGPT etc.

157
00:08:38.840 --> 00:08:40.680
to help them do these workflows.

158
00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:42.200
Then they're starting to

159
00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:45.400
actually orchestrate a set of skills to help them run those workflows.

160
00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:49.360
And then where I think we need to get to is actually having agents that have

161
00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:53.840
a sense of taste, coherency, but also understand their own limitations.

162
00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.520
Software engineering from a

163
00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:59.520
product perspective, has also already gone through this right.

164
00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:03.640
If you think about what cursor did, it was very much like line by line assistance

165
00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:06.920
when it started out with C, Cursor has become more than that over time.

166
00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:11.280
Then we went really kind of deep on Claude Code, and now there's OpenCode.

167
00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:14.360
If you're brave enough from a kind of permissions perspective

168
00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:17.360
to run those agents end to end,

169
00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:22.360
but for the actual automation process is harder, right?

170
00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:26.480
You have a much less consistent set of work and training data

171
00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:27.920
to optimize this on.

172
00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:31.880
So you need to automate your process of gathering information from your customers,

173
00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:34.560
from your slack conversations, from your emails.

174
00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:38.280
You need to work out what your human agent control plane is.

175
00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:42.080
How are you going to ensure that you have the right checks and balances

176
00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:46.640
between your agents and your PM so that they can work effectively?

